#{[17592186507419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507419 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2y-8}{3}\\le \\frac{8-2y}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37bbccda-0b14-5e04-9a10-40c81c14c83e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507419 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-8}{3} & \\le \\frac{8-2y}{4} \\\\ 12\\cdot (\\frac{2y-8}{3}) & \\le 12\\cdot (\\frac{8-2y}{4}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (2y-8) & \\le 3\\cdot (8-2y) \\\\ 8y-32 & \\le 24-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y-32 & \\le 24-6y \\\\ 8y-32+6y & \\le 24-6y+6y \\\\ 14y-32 & \\le 24 \\\\ 14y-32+32 & \\le 24+32 \\\\ 14y & \\le 56 \\\\ \\frac{14y}{14} & \\le \\frac{56}{14} \\\\ y & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{1-7x}{5}\\ge 2.$\n\nThe solution is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41e3b8c9-bf8c-5f28-a82e-0ab2c9c99da3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507426 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-7x}{5} & \\ge 2 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{5}) & \\ge 5\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{\\cancel{5}}) & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x & \\ge 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-7x & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x-1 & \\ge 10-1 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{-7x}{-7} & \\,\\,\\le \\,\\,\\frac{9}{-7} \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -\\frac{9}{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507437 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $3-y>2y+5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f01d823-c35d-5548-8b98-ed400d817eaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507437 #edb/ref 1074 "We solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-y & >2y+5 \\\\ 3-y-2y & >2y+5-2y \\\\ 3-3y & >5 \\\\ 3-3y-3 & >5-3 \\\\ -3y & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the multiplication principle.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y & >2 \\\\ \\frac{-3y}{-3} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-3} \\\\ y & <-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1.$\n\nThe solution is $t$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88277403-5532-598b-ac5e-1cdba1716886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507456 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{2}) & \\le 2\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{\\cancel{2}}) & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6 & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t-6 & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6+6 & \\le 2+6 \\\\ 5t & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{5t}{5} & \\le \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ t & \\le \\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\le \\frac{8}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507467 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4y-9}{15}<\\frac{y}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fd93fd8-c1db-5a78-b0d6-62c128a57e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507467 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y-9}{15} & <\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{4y-9}{15}) & <15\\cdot (\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ 4y-9 & <5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & <5y \\\\ 4y-9-5y & <5y-5y \\\\ -9-y & <0 \\\\ -9-y+9 & <0+9 \\\\ -y & <9 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-1} \\\\ y & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2-3p}{4}<2p+1.$\n\nThe solution is $p$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a763aa0a-1786-546d-877a-3f8cb8ed771a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507475 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-3p}{4} & <2p+1 \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{4}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{\\cancel{4}}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ 2-3p & <8p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3p & <8p+4 \\\\ 2-3p-8p & <8p+4-8p \\\\ 2-11p & <4 \\\\ 2-11p-2 & <4-2 \\\\ -11p & <2 \\\\ \\frac{-11p}{-11} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-11} \\\\ p & >-\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p>-\\frac{2}{11}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507477 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2a-4}{8}\\le \\frac{2a}{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8e1188e-48d5-534b-bcfd-6a2d0af01921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507477 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-4}{8} & \\le \\frac{2a}{6} \\\\ 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a-4}{8}) & \\le 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a}{6}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (2a-4) & \\le 4\\cdot 2a \\\\ 6a-12 & \\le 8a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-12 & \\le 8a \\\\ 6a-12-8a & \\le 8a-8a \\\\ -12-2a & \\le 0 \\\\ -12-2a+12 & \\le 0+12 \\\\ -2a & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ a & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge -6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2z+9}{3}\\ge -z-1.$\n\nThe solution is $z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "beefe539-fabd-5c70-98d6-47fa9012204c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507491 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z+9}{3} & \\ge -z-1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ 2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3 \\\\ 2z+9+3z & \\ge -3z-3+3z \\\\ 5z+9 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5z+9-9 & \\ge -3-9 \\\\ 5z & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{-12}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge -\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge -\\frac{12}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{5z+2}{3}\\ge 4.$\n\n$z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf4150db-c6d9-5a42-a567-768a11a0a924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507503 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z+2}{3} & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2 & \\ge 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2-2 & \\ge 12-2 \\\\ 5z & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{10}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{y+2}{3}<\\frac{y+3}{2}.$\n\nThe solution is $y$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5194de3-9348-5e70-86f0-2dc5eae75238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507506 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{3} & <\\frac{y+3}{2} \\\\ 6\\cdot (\\frac{y+2}{3}) & <6\\cdot (\\frac{y+3}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (y+2) & <3\\cdot (y+3) \\\\ 2y+4 & <3y+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+4 & <3y+9 \\\\ 2y+4-3y & <3y+9-3y \\\\ 4-y & <9 \\\\ 4-y-4 & <9-4 \\\\ -y & <5 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{5}{-1} \\\\ y & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507516 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $4u-11\\ge 9u+4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dcf98c54-1401-5d68-8eaa-0aa383d87a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507516 #edb/ref 1074 "We solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-11 & \\ge 9u+4 \\\\ 4u-11-9u & \\ge 9u+4-9u \\\\ -11-5u & \\ge 4 \\\\ -11-5u+11 & \\ge 4+11 \\\\ -5u & \\ge 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the multiplication principle.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5u & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{-5u}{-5} & \\,\\,\\le \\,\\,\\frac{15}{-5} \\\\ u & \\le -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
